// Rites compile to C functions named after the rite and calls bind by that
// name, so a rite name defined twice in one program (a redefinition, or an
// inner rite shadowing an outer one) produced invalid C or a call bound to the
// wrong rite (looping forever). It is now a transpile error. Found by AFL++.
RITE negate(x) { BEQUEATH 0 - x; }
RITE negate(x) { BEQUEATH 1 - x; }
UTTER(negate(5));
THIS.DIE();
